<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Nesiotic League</h1><p class="st-meta">314 BCE – 167 BCE (approx.) · capital Delos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">League of the Cycladic islands centred on Delos, created by Antigonus I and used by Hellenistic kings to control the Aegean.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 314 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a>, seeking support in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> against rival successors of Alexander, organised the islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> into a league with its headquarters at the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>. Members celebrated festivals named after Antigonus and his son Demetrius.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Delegates of the islands met in a federal council (synedrion) on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>, voted honours for royal patrons and handled common affairs, but real power lay with the protecting king. After the collapse of Demetrius, the league passed under Ptolemaic control in the 280s BCE; officials called nesiarchs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philocles-king-of-sidon">Philocles, king of Sidon</a>, acted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II</a>, and the islanders founded the Ptolemaia festival.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ptolemaic power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> waned in the mid-3rd century BCE and the league faded. It was revived around 200 BCE under Rhodian leadership, but ended in 167 BCE when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and declared it a free port, ending Rhodian dominance.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nesiotic-league.jpg" alt="Nesiotic League" width="320" height="260"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyclades.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>314 BCE – 167 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Delos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antigonus I Monophthalmus, Ptolemy II Philadelphus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion (cult of Apollo)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>League of the Islanders, Koinon ton Nesioton</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-240&amp;focus=nesiotic-league" aria-label="Nesiotic League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5312 832 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nesiotic League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5632 1088L5703 1128M5632 1088L5638 1098M5632 1088L5631 1099M5632 1088L5632 1088M5632 1088L5772 1297M5632 1088L5622 1073M5632 1088L5608 1081"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5703 1128h0M5638 1098h0M5631 1099h0M5632 1088h0M5772 1297h0M5622 1073h0M5608 1081h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5632 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5632 1088h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Delos. <a href="/atlas?year=-240&amp;focus=nesiotic-league">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

